//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// Every iteration of a proved-parallel loop runs exactly once when the host
// spreads it across workers.
//
// The host used to run an outlined kernel on one thread. It now splits the
// loop, giving worker w the contiguous range [w*chunk, (w+1)*chunk). This runs
// the program and checks the answer, which is the only way to find out whether
// the split covers the iteration space exactly -- no iteration left to no
// worker, and none handed to two.
//
// THE BODY ACCUMULATES RATHER THAN ASSIGNS, and that is the whole reason this
// fixture is shaped the way it is. With `o[i][j] = value`, running a slice
// twice writes the same number twice and the total is unchanged, so a split
// that double-booked a range would pass. Adding makes both failures visible in
// one number: a range nobody ran leaves its rows at zero and the total falls, a
// range run twice counts twice and it rises.
//
// That is also why the tensor is zeroed in an ordinary loop first. Accumulating
// into `uninit()` would make the expected total depend on whatever the
// allocator left behind.
//
// THE WORKER COUNTS ARE PINNED rather than left to the machine. The chunk is
// `ceil(trip / workers)`, and rounding it down instead would leave the tail of
// the range to nobody -- but only when the worker count does not divide the
// trip. An earlier version of this fixture ran at the default count, which on
// the 16-CPU machine it was written on divided 64 iterations evenly, so both
// roundings gave a chunk of 4 and it passed with the rounding deliberately
// broken. 3, 5 and 8 none of them divide 67, so each RUN line below fails if
// the rounding is wrong, on any machine.
//
// 1 worker is checked too, and is the load-bearing one: it is the degeneracy
// the whole change rests on, where chunk is the whole trip and the loop is
// exactly the serial one this path ran before.
//
// 67 rows of 8, so the total is the sum of 0..535.
//
// Flat path only. The legacy AST codegen emits none of the markers the split
// reads, so there it stays serial -- correct, and not what this is testing.
//
// REQUIRES: flat-codegen
// RUN: env VX_HOST_THREADS=1 vxc %s 2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s
// RUN: env VX_HOST_THREADS=3 vxc %s 2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s
// RUN: env VX_HOST_THREADS=5 vxc %s 2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s
// RUN: env VX_HOST_THREADS=8 vxc %s 2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s
// RUN: env VX_HOST_THREADS=67 vxc %s 2>/dev/null | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: 143380

fn probe() -> f32 {
  let mut o = Tensor<f32, [67, 8]>::uninit();
  for i in 0..67 {
    for j in 0..8 {
      o[i][j] = 0.0;
    }
  }
  spawn on(Topology::GPU) {
    for i in 0..67 {
      for j in 0..8 {
        o[i][j] = o[i][j] + ((i * 8 + j) as f32);
      }
    }
  }
  let mut acc = 0.0;
  for i in 0..67 {
    for j in 0..8 {
      acc = acc + o[i][j];
    }
  }
  return acc;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
  print(probe());
  return 0;
}
